\begin{abstract}
Models are very large and expensive to run. Much work has been done to procedurally reduce the computational cost of LLMs whilst maintaining performance. In this work, we focus on structured pruning via dropping layers. Many recent works show the presence of redundancy in LLMs. Recent works show some of this redundancy can be detected by static metrics such as angular cosine distance which captures the hidden state drift. Other works use learnable modules to detect where this redundancy will cause least damage to downstream performance. Some works use model confidence as a proxy for task difficulty and use it as an indicator of the ideal pruning regime. Although these 


Does the importance of layers vary based on the domain pertaining to the task.

\end{abstract}


