\section{Motivation}

Dynamic layer pruning: an adpative algorithm which is able to determine the optimal pruning configuration given a task pertaining to a particular distribution and difficulty.

Existing work provides a lack of clarity on how layer importance varies for different tasks.


\textbf{Story directions}

\begin{itemize}
    \item Write the characteristics that would be ideal for a pruning.
    \item Then gradually introduce each of the components of the method, satisfying that characteristic, and then build from there.
    \item \cite{siddiqui2024deeper} showed emulated update works well. The lora adapters would essentially learn what those emulated updates should be. but the risk here is that it would be prone to overfitting. Similarly \cite{gromov2025the} showed cosine similarity acts as a predictor for layer redundancy. Looking at the difference between shapely, which could be considered as an upper bound, and cosine distance, it is not necessary that cosine distance is necessarily providing the optimal path through the transformer to maximize performance and minimize compute costs. But, \cite{siddiqui2024deeper} showed that shapely doesnt generalize well. So maybe cosine distance is better overall. See if you can twist this into a motivation anyway though.
    \item Motivation of using layer drop: Both reduces post-training time while improving inference performance upon layer pruning, while acting as a regularizer (but this needs to be shown empirically).
\end{itemize}

