\section{Introduction}

\textcolor{red}{It is commonly believed that human cognition (Stanovich and West, 2000; Levy, 2008) uses varying cognitive efforts to operate and learn depending on the ‘hardness’ of the input. Specifically, one may only need small efforts (lower computational cost) to process ‘easy’ examples, like the commonly used stop-words, punctuation, patches in the background of an image, etc., but allow additional efforts (more computational cost) for ‘hard’ examples, e.g., a rare abstract concept for reasoning, when they are truly needed. Therefore, allocating the same computational power of a large model uniformly for processing all samples tend to be wasteful and less efficient.}

\textcolor{red}{Overparameterized transformer networks have obtained state of the art results in various natural language processing tasks, such as machine translation, language modeling, and question answering. These models contain hundreds of millions of parameters, necessitating a large amount of computation and making them prone to overfitting. \citep{Fan2020Reducing}}

Much work has been done on unstructured pruning, head pruning. Recently more works have looked into layer pruning, other works look into token-level layer pruning, but this still constitutes a smaller portion of approaches focusing on reducing the number of layers. Many recent works look follow the approach of static pruning approaches where they determine the relevance of a layer by measuring the angular distance between a layer's input and output. The intuition here is that, the lesser the transformations brought about to the hidden state by a layer, the less impact that layer will have on model performance. Recent works have shown that a significant portion of the layers of modern LLMs can be pruned without too much loss in performance due to the slowly changing nature of embeddings across layers, which hypothesized to occur due to the presence of residual skip connections (dejavu).


Ideal characteristics for model sparsity methods \citep{pmlrliu23am}:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Should not require retraining.
  \item Preserve quality.
  \item Lead to measurable inference speedups.
\end{itemize}



While different kinds of pruning methods do enable for more efficiency without too much degradation in performance, the amount of performance degradation is task-dependent. Deja uses contextual (or task specific) information to determine what to prune. Rather, it DejaVu uses an algorithm which takes a look at each layer's inputs to see what to prune.


Unstructured sparsity generally doesn't bring speed ups unless it's highly sparse. Some form of structured sparsity like block pruning will be more useful towards clock time speedups.

Possible experiments to run:

\begin{itemize}
  \item See the effects of different pruning methods across different tasks.
  \item Maybe look into a fairtune like method for task specific pruning.
  \item Benefit of the static mask approach as opposed the dynamic sample specific approach: In the dynamic approach, the full model always needs to be loaded onto the GPU.
\end{itemize}

Goal: Minimize difference between sparse computation and full computation.


