\subsection{Depth-Adaptive Transformer}

This method determines how much computational capacity should be used for each sample, to ensure a certain level of performance \textcolor{purple}{Would be a useful mode to have in our static pruning method}. 


This method is built on the notion that large models are required to do better on hard examples, small models are likely to perform as well on easy ones, e.g., the aforementioned ensemble is probably not required to translate a short phrase such as "Thank you". However, current models apply the same amount of computation regardless of whether the input is easy or hard. \textcolor{purple}{But is it really so that a higher pruning percent will lead more performance degredation on harder samples than easier samples? These works determine the difficulty of a sample using uncertainty. This is however an unreliable way to track sample difficulty due to the potential of model over or under-confidence. Recent works have introduced benchmarks such as E2H, where the difficulty of each sample directly accompanies each sample from the data-loader. This would be a novel form of analysis on the effect of pruning ratios on downstream performance based on task difficulty.}


They carry out dynamic layer selection on both an entire sequence level and token level basis. 


Choosing a different output classifier at each block (anytime prediction) results in an exponential number of possible output classifier combinations in the sequence length.



\subsection{LoRAPrune}

This work aims to look into an iterative structured pruning approach (channel and head pruning) (\textcolor{purple}{In essence, our work is also an iterative structured pruning approach, where, due to layer dropout, random structures are pruned at each iteration. The difference here is that those pruned structures are restored following that training step, and another set of structures are pruned at the following iteration}). At this level of structured pruning, since the lora matrices are added over the entirety of the weights of the model, this renders the zero'd out structured set of weights useless. Additionally, unstructured sparse models are not compatible with lora. The task of separating tuning and pruning has been shown to produce suboptimal results \cite{molchanov2017pruning,sanh2020movementpruningadaptivesparsity} (\textcolor{purple}{Motivating our end-to-end solution. Moreover, our method bypasses the need complex solutions to adapt the low rank adapter as done in this method}). LoRAPrune is proposed as a unified solution for post-training and finetuning. However, LoRAPrune utilizes the moving average of the gradients of the lora modules to determine weight importance. This is a faster way than using the gradients of the full weights to determine structural importance.\textcolor{purple}{This is a more roundabout, expensive and difficult way to determine structural importance than having the model to learn directly in the post-training criterion as is done in our method.} Additionally, all of this is carried out using only a small calibration dataset. Their structured pruning approach matches other semi-structured pruning approaches (\textcolor{red}{do these approaches involve finetuning?}).


Other importance measures: Importance calculated via Taylor expansion is very expensive. 

\subsection{Row-based Sparse Finetuning}

Use lora with sparse training. May be worth looking into: We propose two variants of the Taylor importance for different settings in image and language tasks: classaware Taylor and Zeroth-Order Taylor. The first one is designed for tasks where class-wise accuracy is important (in addition to overall accuracy), such as image classification. Zeroth-Order Taylor is designed for large language models and requires memory only equal to that of a forward pass. In addition, we show how to effectively reduce the estimation variance of the Zeroth-Order estimator. Look into the loss function term used to induce sparsity here.


