\subsection{Adaptive Sparse Training \cite{huang2025pruning}}

Tailored for semi-structured (2:4) sparse models.
KD improves retraining efficiency and enhances model performance. They note that both the mask and weights need to be trained for optimal performance. Adaptive Sparse Trainer, applies sparsity to unimportant weights, while adhering the guard rails for n:m sparsity. They introduce a gradual decay scheduler (Annealing SR-STE): to accelerate convergence and improve performance. Initialization plays a role. Negligible drop in PPL and accuracy in zero-shot tasks. Training free methods usually fall short on zero-shot performance (\textcolor{purple}{check for cosine similarity performance on llm harness zero shot}). They further combine their semi-structured pruning method with AWQ.


Very similar in spirit to our paper: simultaneously trains the lora adapters and the remaining pruned weights. However, they incorporate lora after pruning. While our method does it on the fly (a big difference and a more end-to-end solution).


Sparse Training: mask training purely with cross entropy loss can result in discarding or loss of significant information from the pruned weights. This would be prevented with a KD loss. Solely using language modelling during retraining also brings up the risk of the sparse model being trapped in a local minima. A KD loss helps guide the model towards the local optimum. Their sparse training method maintains all model weights (frozen?), and gradually decreases non-essential weights to zero. \textcolor{purple}{Should have a sort of motivation for loss on the layer-based sparse training of our method.}


Look into the following sparse loss. (However, selecting the appropriate decay strength is challenging. If the decay signal is too strong, masked weights will struggle to regenerate, leading to an almost fixed mask. On the other hand, if the decay signal is too weak, the model will fail to filter out important weights, resulting in heavy oscillation that hinders model convergence. To address this dilemma between stability and exploration, we propose adding a moving decay schedule, with weaker decay strength at the beginning to encourage the model to explore various connectivity patterns and a stronger decay signal toward the end to facilitate model convergence.)


Significant improvement in convergence when using kd loss. When retraining a pruned model,  the initialization from the pre-trained model can result in the model being trapped in a local minima during retraining. They hypothesize that this may be due to overfitting. This local minima dilemma is prevalent in smaller models. So the kd loss is weighted higher in these models. They claim that intermediate KD hinders model’s ability to generalize.


May be worth looking into their sparse low rank boosting (SLoRB). using intermediate information during the distillation of generative language models is detrimental, and that KL loss is sufficient for optimal performance.



\subsection{RoSA}

They propose to combine sparse training with LoRA, since dense finetuning computationally expensive. They claim that RoSA outperforms LoRA and vanilla sparse training. In certain cases, RoSA matches performance of FFT (dense finetuning). They claim that LoRA fails to recover performance on harder finetuning tasks relative to FFT, and this is particularly relevant on harder fine-tuning tasks. RoSA (Robust Adaptation): Similar computational and memory cost relative to LoRA, while being more performant. They do a low-rank + sparse matrix finetuning, inspired by PCA, where the motivation is that matrices arising from noisy measurements (tough problems) can be approximated as a sum of a low-rank component and a sparse one. They use some sort of l0 norm on the sparse mask while finetuning.

\subsection{Sparse Finetuning}

Previous works \cite{sanh2020movementpruningadaptivesparsity} have applied high levels of weight sparsity when finetuning BERT models, where the pretrained model is adapted to a "downstream" task such as question answering or text classification.

The following work by Kutic et al. \cite{kurtic2023sparsefinetuninginferenceacceleration} aims to study efficient sparse fine-tuning on LLMs.

They summarize their work as follows:

\begin{itemize}
    \item Naive sparse fine-tuning, dense fine-tuning while gradually imposing sparsity, causes instability in training. Manifesting itself as 1) loss spikes at higher sparsity values, leading to divergence, 2) dense fine-tuning on small amount of fine-tuning data available for a particular sub-tasks \textcolor{purple}{motivating the use of broader post-training datasets}, 3) overfitting due to dense finetuning on limited data.
    \item This work proposes to first obtain a pruned model using SparseGPT. The sparse finetuning is done by incoorporating the standard cross-entropy loss, output knowledge distillation, and a type of per-token $l_{2}$ knowledge distillation.
    \item They obtain inference speed up via n:m sparse format.
\end{itemize}


\subsubsection{Methodology}

\textbf{Sparse Fine-tuning}

This work follows the paradigm of iterative pruning (with sparse gpt) and finetuning, gradually increasing sparsity levels.

\textbf{Distillation Strategies}

Dense sparse fine-tuning of LLMs is shown to be notoriously difficult. To stabilise this training, KD is used to enable the pruned model to mimic the full teacher model. The most common KD strat is to add a loss term to KL divergence between student and teacher outputs. This work obtains better results by distilling intermediate representations \textcolor{purple}{Does it make sense to distill only the intermediate representation, prior to the LM head, since it is the final LM head layer which makes the ultimate decision}. This work uses MSE loss between intermediate representations (they call this SquareHead). They add a normalization term to stabilize training, as the magnitude of activations may vary significantly across layers. They discard terms corresponding to padding tokens. They advocate this loss as it is consistently performant. 

\textbf{Inference Speedup}

It is difficult to obtain wall-clock speed up using unstructured sparsity methods. They use uniform sparsity patterns such as n:m sparsity on compatible hardware to realize actual runtime speedup.


\textbf{Takeaway from Results}

Cross entropy and standard KD is unstable for full model sparse training, particularly at sparsity rates over 50\%. On generative tasks, they claim that sparse finetuning with squarehead loss at low sparsity levels leads to performance improvements. They show that the method works a bit differently at different bit precision (post-training quantization). \textcolor{purple}{It is worthwhile to run experiments with our method on models at different precision.}


