\subsection{SLEB: Redundancy verification and block elimination}

Prunes transformer block as the fundamental unit to be pruned. Dejavu is a dynamic pruning algorithm assesses whether to bypass certain segments of a layer operation, based on context of the input in single batch scenarios. Early exit dynamically determines whether to skip subsequent transformer blocks, which is usually determined when a model reaches a particular level of confidence. The problem with dynamic pruning is that it requires training to be effective. Early exit methods results in a significant decline in the linguistic capability of the LLMs, as measured by PPL. Shapely-PPL achieved best PPL, and they claim it also increases ppl as blocks are removed. \textcolor{red}{The concept of removing consecutive blocks from LLMs is not effective without dynamic decision-making and training.}

\textbf{Limitations of Early Exit and Dynamic Methods}

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textit{Limited usage in batch settings}: in token-based dynamic pruning settings. Each sample and each token in a batch may require different optimal paths. This complicates implementation and diminishes efficiency benefits in multi-batch contexts.
    \item \textit{Inability to reduce memory requirements}: In dynamic pruning methods, the entire model needs to be loaded onto memory.
    \item \textit{Resource-intensive training}: Dynamic early exit methods typically will end up exiting at one of the final layers in LLMs. The LLM would also require some training process to adapt the LLM to the skip predictor.
\end{itemize}


\textbf{Strengths of SLEB \textcolor{purple}{Which align with ours}}

\begin{itemize}
    \item Inference speed up from structured pruning
    \item Acceleration in multi-batch settings unlike dynamic methods
    \item Reduction gpu memory requirements relative to dynamic methods
\end{itemize}


\subsubsection{Method}

Cosine Similarity: Measure distance between input-outputs on a token level (should check out code). 

\textcolor{red}{These results reveal that, although the similarity between transformer blocks that are further apart varies across the LLM architecture, there is a consistently high degree of similarity between neighboring transformer blocks. This insight underscores the potential redundancy within these models and highlights areas where efficiency could be enhanced through strategic block elimination.  Indeed, this observation emphasizes a fundamental misalignment between existing early exit methods and the nature of LLMs. The primary limitation of early exit methods is their tendency to skip a continuous sequence of transformer blocks. This approach does not take into account the high degree of output similarity between adjacent transformer blocks, as indicated by our cosine similarity measurements. Consequently, such strategies risk bypassing blocks that are essential for preserving the linguistic integrity of the LLMs.}

Metric 1: Cosine distance

Metric 2: Shapely-PPL

The removal of a continuous sequence of blocks has a more significant impact on performance.


Broad Methodology: Measure cosine distance. Remove block. Recalculate consine distance. Remove block.




\subsection{LLM-Streamline}

This work proposes a method to replace redundant layers (as determined by cosine similarity). They use a lightweight feedforward network as a replacement and is trained by taking the MSE between the output of the layer to be replaced and the lgihtweight fully connected layer. This work involves removing \textit{consecutive layers} \textbf{\textcolor{purple}{This is a deviation from our work where the model hints at which layers are more or less important via L1 norm, offering a more dynamic(?) path. Though the direction of pruning consecutive layers is much easier since you can insert a single module as a replacement. Though maybe with layer dropout, lora reparameterizes the layers such that this is not a problem. But the main question is, is the representation power and capacity of LoRa enough to make up for the hidden state drift arising from pruned layers. Maybe it is worthwhile attaching a lightweight linear layer after or before each layer, and to play around with whether this linear should or should be dropped. Unlike LLM-Streamline, which divides the task of layer selection and layer replacement, our solution would be combine these process into one end-to-end layer pruning and replacement/healing solution. It needs to work better, then we call on the motivation end-to-end deep learning solutions always being the ultimate and best performing and widely used methodologies. And this can inspire further research in this direction.}} 


Works well and makes sense if irrelevant layers are consecutive. Since it replaces a blocks of layers. The loss is mse. Doesnt it make more sense to use KL? What is the relevance of Swiglu. Cosine works well because it is agnostic to magnitude of vectors.

Rationale of replacing layers with light weight mlp: the transformations carried out by layers indicated by cosine as low, can be captured by low capacity networks.

Rationale for MLP over LoRA: 
1. Finetuning the model using PEFT requires storing of the weights, activation values and optimizer state of the PEFT  module, but this is not that expensive no?

2. Layer replacement involves 2 stages. Dataset construction and model training. The dataset here are the input-output embeddings of the selected consecutive blocks. So no training with the big model. Just storing the model weights and activation values.. Soo it doesnt involve storing the activation state (for gradients) and optimizer state. The second stage involves storing lightweight network’s weight, activation values and optimizer state. This ends up being cheaper

Other Notes: They speculate that LoRA is a process of redistributing the function of the pruned layer across the other layers (which they say is a harder task). But it doesn’t tho, it redistributes it along the lora modules. While their layer replacement strategy follows straightforward knowledge distillation. 


Evaluation Metrics:
They say the problem with accuracy is that they measure for answer correctness, which the model can get correct even if the confidence of the model is way off. They want to measure for consistency.
Additionally they also show results as a function of maintained performance.

Process of MCQ eval: . The input to the classification task consists of a question accompanied by multiple choices, and the LLM is required to select the correct answer from these choices. Typically, each choice is concatenated with the question to form multiple sentences, and the perplexity (PPL) of each sentence is computed. The choice corresponding to the sentence with the lowest PPL is selected as the answer. 

They note that on certain MCQ benchmarks, the performance improves as pruning occurs. From analysis on the confusion matrix, they observe that confidence of the pruned model is significantly worse, and the accuracy overestimates the performance of the model. Their proposed stability metric checks for consistency of model confidence before and after pruning.

Calibration set: SlimPajama - data from different domains. 30000 sequences.

Results: Their method performs better on accuracy. And performs better by an even bigger margin on the consistency metric. Even outperorming certain structured pruning methods that prune more fine-grained structures.

This paper emphasizes maintaining the soundness of the hidden state. Which is the same motivation for our work.

The FFN works better than a transformer layer as a replacement layer. Atleast , the FFN layer converges faster than the transformer layer.

FFN vs LoRA: They use a rank of 128 on LoRA, to match the parameters of FFN. FFN performs better, while needing less compute. BUT NOT BY MUCH. Limited running.

Other notable results: 
shapely (PPL) overfits more than cosine.
Scaling post-training dataset from 30000 to 6B improves performance slightly but at 100x the cost.

What about results at higher sparsity: They dont go above 25%

Very similar in spirit. Very important baseline.



\textcolor{red}{The core idea of layer pruning is to identify and remove less important layers in an LLM. Specifically, the effect of each layer can be viewed as a transformation of the hidden states. If the input and output hidden states of a particular layer are highly similar, such as exhibiting high cosine similarity, we can say that the layer has a small impact on adjusting the hidden states. As illustrated in Fig. 2, our pilot study shows that certain contiguous layers indeed have smaller impact on the hidden states, indicating they are less important and suitable for pruning. Some concurrent works (Song et al., 2024; Kim et al., 2024; Yang et al., 2024; Men et al., 2024; Gromov et al., 2024) also explore layer pruning. These studies either prune unimportant layers directly without further training (Song et al., 2024; Men et al., 2024) or fine-tune the pruned model to enhance performance (Kim et al., 2024; Yang et al., 2024; Gromov et al., 2024). Directly removing layers can lead to more performance degradation. While parameter-efficient fine-tuning techniques like LoRA (Hu et al., 2021) are used to train the pruned LLM, fine-tuning the model to make the original non-contiguous layers compensate for the performance degradation is not an easy task (details in Section 2.3).}


