\subsection{Nvidia Prune and Distill}

Owing to their enormous size, computing gradient information on modern LLMs is prohibitively memory and compute-intensive, and one of our primary goals is to avoid this expensive step when trying to obtain importance information. In this paper, we propose a purely activation-based importance estimation strategy that simultaneously computes sensitivity information for all the axes we consider (depth, neuron, head, and embedding channel) using a small (1024 samples) calibration dataset and only forward propagation passes. We now describe how this strategy is implemented for each individual axis.


Iterative cosine distance prune and distill. Calibration set of size 1024

Logit distillation when fewer layers are pruned. Logit-based KD loss across the sequence of all output tokens

Iterative Importance: in this setting, we iteratively alternate between pruning and importance estimation for a given axis or combination of axes. Makes sense because once layers are pruned, the flow of the hidden state will change. Doesnt do any better

KD: train small model to mimic outputs or hidden states. The mismatch in student and teacher hidden states is handled by learning a shared linear transformation during distillation to upscale the student hidden state to the teacher hidden state dimension. The hidden states used are always post LayerNorm.

KD dataset: Unless otherwise specified, we use 1.8 billion tokens (400 steps) from nemotron for lightweight retraining.

We use the Nemotron-4 curated 8 trillion token (8T) base pretraining dataset and the continued training dataset (CT) [42, 44, 43]. We use the 8T training blend for all our ablations and use a combination of both data blends to retrain our final models.

6. Use (logit+intermediate state+embedding) distillation when depth is reduced significantly. 7. Use logit-only distillation when depth isn’t reduced significantly. Does using the first one during sparse training mean that itll will lose less performance at higher sparsities.

9. Perform lightweight retraining to stabilize the rankings of searched pruned candidates. - some sort of healing

Takeaways
1. training one single big model, and obtaining smaller ones from it through pruning + retraining achieves higher accuracy and is extremely cost/compute-efficient when compared to training them from scratch. Further, our efficient retraining strategy also eliminates the need to curate trillions of tokens of data.

Loss function: Choice of Loss Function: we experiment with Kullback-Leibler divergence (KLD), MSE, cosine similarity and reverse KLD (R-KLD) to compute Llogits. Recent work has shown R-KLD [15, 27] to be a better fit than KLD in the SFT/instruction-following setting, and Agarwal et al. [2] claim the choice of loss is task-dependent. We observe from Table 15 and 16 that KLD is the best choice for pruned base model training.

Choice of Losses: typically, a weighted combination of LCLM and Llogits is used for distillation. We find that using Llogits alone results in the best performance as shown in Table 15. For $Lis = Lemb + Latt + Li + Lo$ , we make several observations similar to Lu et al. [31]; these are listed in Appendix A.4 and in Table 17. Most notably, we observe no improvements from using Lis when retraining models that don’t prune the depth axis significantly, such as MINITRON 8B and MINITRON 4B and hence use Llogits alone in such cases

We hypothesize that aggressive one-shot pruning loses out on important capabilities of the base LLM. We thus explore a simple iterative two-step pruning and retraining strategy where we first prune and retrain Nemotron-4 15B to create MINITRON 8B (46\% reduction) and further prune and retrain the latter to MINITRON 4B (50\% reduction).  – Dropout plus kd is essentially an iterative prune and training solution – but it is an end to end sparse training solution for robust layer pruning

\subsection{Dynamic Routing / Contextual Sparsity}

A router (can be some form of linear layer) which dynamically decides the computational path based on the input. This method follows the intuition that different inputs require varying computational demands.

\subsubsection{DejaVu}
Contextual sparsity refers to a smaller subset of the model which is chosen in an input-dependent manner, that yeilds approximately the same output as the dense model \citep{pmlrliu23am}. Given inputs to each layer, DejaVu \citep{pmlrliu23am} predicts sparsity of the proceeding layer on the fly with negligible drop in performance while speeding up inference times. According to Liu et al. \citep{pmlrliu23am}, contextual sparsity can be predicted \textbf{only} with sufficient information from token embeddings. Additionally, contextual sparsity can be predicted based on the \textbf{similarity} between layer parameters (heads/MLPs) and the input tokens to that particular layer. DejaVu \citep{pmlrliu23am} uses a \textit{asynchronous} lookahead predictor to predict where the contextual sparsity lies for the proceeding layer. Particularly, given the input to a layer, DejaVu predicts the relevant attention heads and MLP parameters and only loads these parameters for computation. To achieve wall-clock speed up times, they implement a \textcolor{red}{hard-aware sparse matrix multiply} using CUDA kernel programming. 

DejaVu: Finding contextual sparsity. Two forward passes.
\begin{itemize}
    \item{First forward pass: see which heads and parameters yield high output norms.}
    \item{Second forward pass: Use only these parameters.}
    \item{Both forward passes yield similar results. Their results show that upto 80\% of attention heads and 95\% MLP parameters can be removed.}
    \item{DejaVu: aims to predict where this sparsity lies.}
    \item{DejaVu: A similarity-based prediction algorithm.}
\end{itemize}

DejaVu: Other observations:
\begin{itemize}
    \item{Residuals have high norms. They hypothesize this is due to the presence of high sparsity.}
    \item{\textcolor{blue}{My take: This could also the other way around. The model somehow learned to concentrate a lot of the important processing towards the start of the neural network. This was enabled due to the residual connections present in the model which pass along the information via skip connections.}}
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Contextual Sparsity in MLP blocks}

Contextual sparsity can be accurately computed from the output activation of the layer. The challenge is to bring actual efficiency and predict where this sparsity is likely to occur. The prediction method needs to be \textit{fast} and \textit{precise}. For each MLP block, they use a small 2 layer MLP block to predict contextual sparsity, that is, it predicts a set of important neurons.

\textbf{Contextual Sparsity in Attention Blocks}

Only a few attention heads perform important computations. One challenge of sparse prediction in attention blocks is the attention layer's dependence on previous tokens. It is not clear if the previous token's key and value is needed for sparse prediction. It is also not straightfoward on how to handle missing KV cache of past tokens for current token computation.

\textbf{Asynchronous Execution}

So now, there are two additional layers before every existing MLP and MHSA layer. These additional layers can easily diminish any gains made by the predicted pruning method. To prevent this overhead, they use an asynchronous implementation method. So basically, since the embeddings of the layer change very slowly, you are able to use the input of the previous layer to predict the sparsity in the next layer. Essentially, this allows the sparsity prediction MLP and the transformer layers to be computed in parallel, preventing any inference time overhead. But it certainly does involve the use of additional computation, which they argue, just results in better and more complete utilization of hardware. 

\textbf{Hardware-efficient Implementation}

In essence, DejaVu is an unstructured pruning method. It uses cuda kernel optimization tricks using Triton code to bring actual speed up from this unstructured sparsity.


\subsection{SkipLayer}

This paper argues that a variable amount of computation should be allocated on a per-token basis. A binary router is used to predict whether a layer should be skipped. 

\subsubsection{Router Function}
The router function is trained to assign only a subset of the inputs to the proceeding layer to maximize performance according to a specified budget. The router outputs a binary mask matrix. The router takes as input the batch of input sequences of shape $BxTxN$ where $B$ is the batch size, $T$ is the sequence length, and $N$ is the embedding size. The router outputs a binary mask of shape $BxT$, indicating which tokens should be selected. Several design choices can be token for the router.

\textbf{Design option 1: Sigmoid:} One of the design choices that can be taken for the router is using the sigmoid activation function, which bounds the output to a value between zero and one, and is easily differentiable. The paper says an additional decision rule is needed to find a threshold

\textbf{uDesign option 2: Top 1:} A top-1 implementation, that is, two values are output by the router for each token. The two values indicate the scores for whether the token should or should not be selected, and then the argmax (Top-1) of the scores are taken to decide whether to skip or not. However, these hard probabilities are not differntiable. To overcome this problem, previous works soften the scores via blending. This allows gradients to flow while learning. This did not work well because, it didn't enable for the precise control of sparsity, that is, the softening of the scores meant that the tokens were being weighted by a small value and being passed through rather than being completely negated.

\textbf{Design option 3: Straight-Through Gumbel-Softmax:} Basically, it just uses the hard scores during forward propagation and uses the soft scores during backprop while computing gradients.


\subsubsection{Router Training}
The training of the router function is similar to the L1 loss of ViT-Slimming. Basically, a budget is specified prior to training, and then a regularization term is added to the NLL loss to train the model to reach the desired sparsity level.

\subsubsection{Implementation Details}
The amount of tokens skipped at each layer varies, making it a flexible design. However, this can lead to inefficiencies as having varying amount of computation at different layers is an inefficient way to use the GPU. Current hardware prefer fixed and predictable workloads. \textcolor{red}{TODO: Understand efficient implementation} There is some sort of efficient mechanism to grap the non skipped tokens and only perform computation on them. The skipped tokens are completely ignored.

This work considers a layer as including both self attention layer and the feedforward, so it does block skipping rather than individual layer skipping.

\subsubsection{Dealing with KV-cache Issues} It only skips computation of queries on skipped tokens but still computes K and V, in order to retain context for the tokens which are not skipped.

