\subsubsection{Their motivation}

\textbf{Analysis of what happens in each layer}

They use an example prompt from HumanEval coding dataset. When generating each token, they check for what's happening in each layer by exiting at each layer and routing to the classification head. Their analysis shows that earlier layers produce incorrect tokens similar to the input token as they have not been transformed properly yet. There is more entropy in the nature of prediction from middle layers, which they refer to as the LLM being "hesitant and change their minds". They also show the last few layers are unnecessary, and the correct prediction often occurs a few layers before the end. They find that they are able to maintain performance with an average of 23.45 out of the model's 32 layers. The idea is to detect when the predictions (or hidden state?) saturate.


\textcolor{blue}{"By default, deep learning models are not motivated to predict their final output early and instead spread their compute across all layers Voita et al. (2019, 2023)." - This motivates the use of intermediary layer skipping methods rather than early exit, to minimize the impact of hidden state drift. Their method is aimed towards minimizing the reliance of the model on later layers. }

Other works developed predictors to detect where the predictions saturate in order to early exit \cite{lepori2024racingthoughtsexplaininglarge, geva2022transformerfeedforwardlayersbuild}. \textcolor{blue}{This can probably formulated as where the hidden state transformations saturate.} These predictors may be in the form of an additional LM head, or additional modules.

\textcolor{blue}{We would like to adapt the layers to become robust to drifts in the hidden state after layer pruning pruning. This is done via layer drop during post training, reparameterizing the existing layers to hidden state from pruning layers with LoRA, and using knowledge distillation loss from teacher (full model) rather than supervised finetuning to train the adapters to maintain hidden state of the model in response to layer dropping. Additionally, we introduce a adaptive and input dependent router/mask/layer weighting component to let the model decide which layers are deemed most important for the task/prompt at hand.} 

Layer skip combines early exit with speculative decoding to propose a self-speculative decoding approach which requires no additional layers or models. They add a loss functions enable LM heads to more easily "understand" embeddings of earlier layers. \textcolor{blue}{Though this may not be necessary in our case, since we are trying to have the LoRA layers to compensate and adjust for the drift in hidden state. Although, it is unlikely that the LoRA adapters will be able to fully adjust. So it is likely worth a try.}

