\subsection{Speculative Decoding}

A small model is utilized for drafting a proposal prediction and uses the large one to determine whether to accept or reject it. This method relies on the quality of the small model for proposal drafting. A better drafter results in more accepted token acceptance rates and longer speculated sequences. Only suitable for autoregressive models, not suitable for encoder transformers \cite{leviathan2023fastinferencetransformersspeculative, chen2023acceleratinglargelanguagemodel}. From initial understanding, it seems to be more relevant for token pruning. Though, the prediction mechanism is worth looking at.

Speculative decoding results in no drop in accuracy. A large model is referred to as the main model, while a smaller faster model is used to as the draft model. This results in faster inference compared to the original model but increases memory footprint. Further complexity is also added to maintain the KV cache in two different models.

Speculative decoding helps to rapidly generate tokens.

