\subsection{Metric-based Layer Pruning}

Recent works \cite{siddiqui2024deeper,gromov2025the,men2025shortgpt} showed that there exists a large degree of redundancy over the necessity of layers in LLMs. Some works \cite{dejavu_liu23am} attribute this redundancy to the presence of residual connections in modern LLMs which results most of the \textit{crucial} processing being carried out at the earlier layer and while the latter layers result in the embeddings slowly changing. However, it is noted in these works that the last MLP layer connected to the LM head remains crucial to performance, where Elhousi et al.  \cite{Elhoushi_2024} show tuning the LM head to adapt it to the hidden state drift which will occur from pruning can lead to great reduction in performance loss while layer pruning. Other works \cite{men2025shortgpt} note that this redundancy arises when pre-norm transformer blocks are used, that is, layer normalization is placed before the corresponding self-attention and MLP layers. These works propose to detect the most redundant layers by using angular distance to measure how much the embeddings change between the input and output of the layer/block in question.

Gromov et al. \cite{gromov2025the} propose a method of pruning from 

