\subsubsection{Results}

\textbf{Results of Goromov et al. \cite{gromov2025the}}

On MMLU (4 choice MCQ - random baseline $25\%$), performance robust to pruning until about $45\%-55\%$ sparsity, after which a sharp drop off in performance is observed. But this point of performance drop off varies based on model family. The performance drops of faster $20\%-30\%$ in the Qwen family of models. The knowledge necessary to solve problems is lost at some particular point, and it is different for different models. \textcolor{red}{Is there some difference between the training schemes of Llama and Qwen which causes this performance drop off?} Finetuning pushes this drop off point to slightly higher levels of sparsity. They claim that larger and deeper models are more robust to layer pruning which they hypothesize is because smaller models are more overtrained \textcolor{red}{but from the results it seems that this drop off point is not really shifted as per percentage of layers dropped, but in terms raw number of layers which can be dropped, more layers can be dropped from larger models till this drop off point is reached}. They claim that finetuning significantly restores performance and robustness to layer pruning, but they show that performance restoration on the very dataset on which they finetuned. But when evaluating on MMLU, this impact is less pronounced. \textcolor{blue}{Bring this point up and show that our sparse training method leads to more pronounced improvements outside the finetuning/healing set (C4 dataset).}

Their claimed generalizations from results: The layers with the smallest angular distance are more pruneable. The last layer should never be pruned \textcolor{red}{directly motivating against early exit, though, in early exit each layer has its own classification head... how much computation overhead do these LM heads add? Not any if the classification head is shared across all layers}. Weird observations in qwen that the points of highest angular distance is more patched like islands across the layers of the model rather than just being focused towards the early layers and final layer. Their main proposed simple pruning strategy: Prune backwards (early exit) except the last layer and then finetune to restore. From the results, it seems that cosine similarity performs better, and after finetuning both perform similarly. Though, their proposed method of pruning requires no additional compute to be performed on a calibration set. Healing leads to lower losses in response to layer pruning, but this does not translate into improved accuracy on MMLU.


\textbf{Results of Siddiqui et al. \cite{siddiqui2024deeper}}

They propose a method for computing adaptive layer importance rankings based on Shapely values (found to be very expensive). They note that this improves performance on the task at hand but reduces generalizability to other tasks.

They can remove 33\% of self-attention layers (so 15\% of overall layers) without a significant drop in performance. They highlight the differing impact which different metrics have on downstream performance on MMLU. They also separately evaluate the importance of MLP and MHSA layers unlike previous work \cite{gromov2025the}. They show that using the additive average of that block performs on par with or better than learning algorithms, because learning algorithms could lead to overfitting or catastrophic forgetting. They use 150k sequences with a context length of 2048 as their calibration set.

\textbf{Results of ShortGPT \cite{men2025shortgpt}}

They note that when they remove 25\% of the layers on 7B models, the performance on certain generative tasks like XSum and C3 decreases to nearly zero. While the performance drop is not that significant on larger 13B models.

