\subsection{Interpretibility Works}

Are factual associations stored across layers with any discernable patterns? And if there is, can some association be formed between these interpretability patterns and pruning layers for some sort of informative evaluation. How does localization of knowledge and problem solving skills work across layers. What's the existing concensus on that? 

\section{Experiments}


\subsection{Datasets}

We want to evaluate on datasets that vary in difficulty, with the intuition that easier problems should require less computation \citep{ding2024easyhardbench}. It is difficult to profile a particular dataset as being easy or hard. It is more likely that certain samples in datasets can be easy, while other samples will be hard. Recent work \citep{ding2024easyhardbench} introduces the Easy2hard-Bench comprising of 6 datasets of varying types, which include E2H-AMC, E2H-Codeforces, C2H-Lichess, E2H-GSM8K, E2H-ARC and E2H-Winogrande. Each problem is annotated with numerical difficulty scores, which are computed using established difficulty ranking systems such as Item Response Theory and Glicko-2 models. This work posits that problem difficulty need not depend on domain nature, rather, samples are determined as difficult if they can only be solved by examinees with sufficient expertise. 

We evaluate the effect of layer pruning on problems segregated into 3 groups- easy, medium and hard- determined by the difficulty assigned in the dataset by the respective metrics \citep{ding2024easyhardbench}.


\subsubsection{Calibration Sets}

Gromov et al (2024) \cite{gromov2025the} did healing with 4-bit quantization QLoRA using C4 with 164M or 328M tokens. No mention about context length. Default size of calibration set in code in Siddiqui et al. \cite{siddiqui2024deeper} is 1000000 with a context size of 1024 and they use the MMLU dataset directly to compute shapely importance on MMLU.


\subsection{Baselines}

\begin{itemize}
    \item Cosine metric \cite{men2025shortgpt,gromov2025the}
    \item Cosine metric + QLoRA on remaining blocks \cite{gromov2025the}
    \item Cosine + emulated update \cite{siddiqui2024deeper}: Additive bias. Compute empirical additive mean of a particular layer. Calculate the average difference between the input and output of a layer over a calibration set. 
    \item Iterative MMLU Shapely \cite{siddiqui2024deeper}
    \item Attention Only \cite{siddiqui2024deeper}: Significant because it reduces the burden of KV cache.
    \item Early Exit \cite{gromov2025the}
    \item LoRA Adapter In placed of the pruned block \cite{siddiqui2024deeper}: Three different ways of training. 1. Minimize MSE between output of pruned block and low rank adapter. 2. SFT on final model output. 3. logit distillation on model output.
    \item Relative L1
    \item Relative L2
    \item Shapely Value \cite{siddiqui2024deeper}: It is computed by calculating the marginal loss in performance in the absence of that layer. They consider this an upper bound. (\textcolor{red}{Very similar to the PPL importance used.})
    \item Layer Importance on Calibration (Cheating)
    \item Relative Magnitude: Assumes layers with larger $\left\|\frac{f(x)}{x+f(x)}\right\|$ is of higher importance.
    \item \textcolor{red}{Compute average inverse cosine similarity?}
\end{itemize}

